      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The relative contributions of psychosocial stress and psychiatric morbidity to suicide are a subject of debate."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIMS",
          "content": "To determine major risk factors for suicide in rural south India."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "We used a matched case-control design and psychological autopsy to assess 100 consecutive suicides and 100 living controls matched for age, gender and neighbourhood."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Thirty-seven (37%) of those who died by suicide had a DSM-III-R psychiatric diagnosis. Alcohol dependence (16%) and adjustment disorders (15%) were the most common categories. The prevalence rates for schizophrenia, major depressive episode and dysthymia were 2% each. Ongoing stress and chronic pain heightened the risk of suicide. Living alone and a break in a steady relationship within the past year were also significantly associated with suicide."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Psychosocial stress and social isolation, rather than psychiatric morbidity, are risk factors for suicide in rural south India."
        }
      ]},
